 We have clarified how \tool{} differs from and complements SmartFuzz in Section~VI, and we have extended the comparison in Section~IV. SmartFuzz uses LLMs to synthesize new seeds from features extracted from official DBMS documentation and historical bug-triggering inputs, whereas \tool{} transfers existing test cases from mature DBMSs, which cover a broad range of SQL features, and preserves their cross-statement structures. The two approaches therefore provide complementary seeds: SmartFuzz targets bug-prone features, whereas \tool{} provides broad feature coverage.
 
-To compare them under the same setting, we use the seeds released by SmartFuzz and run them with \griffin{} on all four target DBMSs, with the same DBMS versions, resource allocation, campaign duration, and coverage measurement as the other tools in RQ2. As shown in Table~III and Table~IV below, \tool{} covers 422,130 branches and detects 22 bugs in total, compared with 394,825 branches and 17 bugs for SmartFuzz, i.e., 7\% more branches and 5 more bugs. On Virtuoso, SmartFuzz achieves slightly higher coverage than \tool{}, and both detect 3 bugs.
